{
  "requirement": "Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST jointly give the direction that the requirements on them specify. A skill consists of its `SKILL.md` and the files under its `references/` directory. Each topic MUST be stated in full in exactly one skill, its home.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations in `requirements.cli.skill_structure`:**\n1. The bundled skills jointly give the direction the requirements on them specify. This is delegated to the refining requirements `requirements.cli.skill_structure.habeas`, `.requirements`, `.evidence`, `.bootstrap`, `.refactor` and `.refer`. Each has its own tests and review, so it needs no test here.\n2. A skill is its `SKILL.md` plus the files under its `references/` directory.\n3. Each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill, its home.\n\n**Test coverage.** The test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics]`.\n- **Skill definition (obligation 2).**\n  - The test lists `assets/skills` and asserts the directories equal exactly the five names in `SKILLS`.\n  - For each skill it asserts `SKILL.md` starts with front matter naming that skill.\n  - It compares the files on disk (`SKILL.md` plus every file in `references/`) with the files `skill_files` lists. Those listed files are what `bundled_skill` and the other tests read. A skill therefore means exactly its `SKILL.md` and `references/` files, and an unlisted reference file would fail.\n  - It also asserts that the Symposium manifest publishes `assets/skills`.\n- **Single home (obligation 3).** For every skill, `assert_home` runs.\n  - It asserts that the home's combined text contains each of its `home_markers` phrases.\n  - It asserts that none of the other four skills' combined `SKILL.md` and `references/` text contains any of those phrases.\n  - The markers are distinctive phrases for each home's topics, such as the task routing table in `habeas`, the review criteria in `habeas-requirements`, the three outcomes in `habeas-evidence`, and `--map` in `habeas-refactor`. A topic restated in full in a second skill, or missing from its home, would fail.\n\n**Assessment.**\n- The text gives no finer criterion for \"stated in full\", so the marker-based exclusivity check is a reasonable way to test it. A restatement in entirely different words would pass, but the requirement does not define one.\n- The `.refer` requirement, which allows summaries and requires referral, is delegated to its own requirement and test.\n\nI found no untested obligation, no wrong expectation and no marking gap.",
  "references": "5a5c704ea25b3efceb4b7c4b3d3f998ccd6490c969be6f0fcaaf7011ddc4cf17",
